Z||000004||pherder||08-11-2000||06:30 PM||||DJRice,<P>Yup, I have a 22x24 detached garage that is there to work on my "stuff".  I have air tools and an engine hoist, a couple of jacks and farm of jack stands.<P>I know exactly what has to come off and where to put it, what tool is needed where and exactly the way to disassemble quickly.<P>Going back together takes a little more time cause I want it right going together.  Hey, I might want to drive it!  (yeah, right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )<P>Remember, no time is spent draining the coolant because I do the trick that Archie taught me ... make sure it will run BEFORE you add the coolant.  Now that it runs, I will get the MAP, the IAT and the clutch  working and then I will add the coolant.<P>Wish me luck, I will be diving into the wiring this weekend (again   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT ... All Fiero stuff (including V8 install and ARTERO body kit) for sale ... $15,000 takes all three cars and all the parts I have!!!<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 08-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||pherder||08-11-2000||10:07 PM||||Now I am getting really confused!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>My wiring to the MAP is correct!  The only thing wrong is the IAT is wired backwards ... i.e. the sensor ground (ECM pin) goes to the A terminal of the IAT and the B terminal of the IAT goes to the IAT sensor pin on the ECM.  Since the IAT is just a resistance change due to temperature change, I can't see that hooking it up backwards would change anything?  I will be cutting and rewiring it correctly but I can't reason it out as being the problem.<P>Anyone?<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>Sedgwick, KS<BR>88 Coupe, 88 Formula, 88 GT ... All Fiero stuff (including V8 install and ARTERO body kit) for sale ... $15,000 takes all three cars and all the parts I have!!!||||reg||8
Z||000006||pherder||08-12-2000||01:54 PM||||YES!!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Found the wiring problem.  When everything is "right", disassemble it further.  Found where the two wires were cut and spliced together, one of the splices had a sharp strand that poked thru into the other wire!<P>Found this out by taking the harness apart at the section that goes to the MAP and separating the wires and trying the scan again.  This time I could vary the voltage going to the ECM by sucking on the vacuum line.<P>BTW, the IAT DOES have to be connected one way to make it work.  Swapped the leads and now have the correct temperature reporting to the ECM.  It now reads ~84 degrees F instead of the 304 degrees F it did before.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Now that I have that problem solved, I am working with Rodney Dickman to be able to use a Getrag Slave with a internal levered transaxle (such as the ones that are on a lot of V6 GM engines.)<P>By the end of next week, I hope to have a running, drivable car.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||14
